        "reasoning": "Good: `tool.go:210-217` — default timeout 120s, but if tool implements `Timeout() time.Duration` and it's **greater** than current timeout, it's raised. `createAgentTool.Timeout()` returns 10min → 600s \u003e 120s → raised to 10min. Good. `askAgentTool` has no Timeout() → 120s default. So a genuine ask to a real LLM has a 120s budget. That's plausible but worth noting: ask_agent嵌套 (A asks B, B asks C) — each level has 120s but the child's 120s is nested inside parent's 120s, so deep chains truncate. Also create_agent with task = 10min while its internal askAgentTool has 120s cap... wait, create_agent calls askAgentTool.Execute directly (not through registry), so no tool timeout wrapper applied there — it uses ctx passed in, which is execCtx of create_agent with 10min. So create+task gets 10min. But ask_agent directly gets 120s. Inconsistent: same wait, different budgets depending on entry point. Minor design smell worth mentioning.\n\nAlso: 120s nested — if parent asks child and child asks grandchild, grandchild's own 120s... each has own execCtx derived from parent's ctx. Fine.\n\nNow write the end-to-end test. Focus: create sub-agent → communicate → verify. Also parallel fan-out with -race.\n\nTest plan (file `internal/agent/subagent_comm_test.go`):\n\n1. `TestSubAgentCreateAndCommunicate`:\n   - m with mock provider\n   - parent a (Backend mock)\n   - create via createAgentTool: name 帮手 role 审查\n   - verify FindByName ok, backend \"loop\", parent's llm/workdir copied (already covered)\n   - set helper backend \"mock\" (to have a real reply path)\n   - ask_agent from parent → StatusOK, content 包含「收到」\n   - verify helper.Messages has user task + assistant reply\n   - verify parent history NOT polluted (parent.messages length unchanged) — because ask goes through tool, not parent's Messages. Actually parent never ran; fine.\n   - verify list_agents sees it\n\n2. `TestSubAgentParallelFanOut`: create 3 helpers (via tool), set each to mock, then 3 goroutines ask in parallel; verify each reply OK and contains its own reply text; run with -race.\n\nWait: mock backend reply content — need to know what mock returns: \"收到\" per existing test. All three would reply the same text, so cross-talk undetectable by content. Better: verify each helper's Messages contain its own task text — that proves no cross-talk. Yes: helper's snapshot should include its own task string.\n\n3. `TestSubAgentAskWhileParentBusy` / deadlock test: A asks B while B is already running a long task → should queue, not deadlock. Use ctx timeout. Might be flaky; skip or make simple.\n\nAlso test: create_agent exceeding maxAgents (8) returns Fail. Existing? not tested. Quick test adds value but creates 8 agents with mock — fine, fast.\n\nLet me also verify a potential bug: `createAgentTool` checks `len(t.m.List()) \u003e= maxAgents` AFTER FindByName — fine.\n\nPotential real bug I noticed: `createAgentTool` doesn't inherit `Backend` from parent. If parent is a CLI-backed agent (claude/codex)... child gets \"loop\" — fine. But if the parent's *provider* is mock, child gets provider mock → loop backend errors → create+task always fails in that setup. Not a production bug (real users have real providers).\n\nAnother real issue: `askAgentTool` returns `tool.Fail` when reply==\"\" and status error — my fix. Good.\n\nOne more: **`createAgentTool.Execute` calls `t.m.SetAgentLLM` and `SetAgentWorkDir` which trigger async `goSave`. Then it immediately calls ask_agent → child may start running before config persists. Not a correctness issue since in-memory.**\n\nBUT: there's a real bug candidate — `Create(\"\", in.Name, in.Role)` then `SetAgentLLM`. Between Create and SetAgentLLM, the child's worker is already running (Create starts worker goroutine). If nothing is queued yet, no problem. But if the child was created and another agent asks it concurrently in that window, it would use default config. Narrow race, low value.\n\nActually here's a REAL one worth testing: **`createAgentTool` has `Timeout() 10min` but `askAgentTool` inside uses the same ctx — fine. However `createAgentTool` marks `Writes() bool { return false }`** — semantics: creating an agent is arguably a write. `Writes()` presumably gates approval prompts / repeat detection. Let me check what Writes() does in tool.go — if Writes=false bypasses approval, that's a security consideration. Let me check quickly.\n\nLet me check Writes() usage then write tests."
