        "timestamp": 1794915773,
        "reasoning": "Now, key questions:\n\n1. Render() call sites: only main.go:604. No test file referencing Transcript.Render. Grep confirmed: knowledge.Render is separate. So changing the signature breaks only main.go:604 — but that's exactly what B fixes. So compile risk is low. Let me double-check with `go build`/`go vet` after hypothetical? Can't modify files. But I can grep in tests for \"Render()\" — none. Also check e2e or frontend includes. Let's grep broader including non-go files (docs, specs) for Render(.\n\n2. self ident: Message.FromID vs FromName. In SendToChannel, fromId and fromName are params. What is fromId when user sends? Let's check frontend calls SendToChannel — fromId \"user\" for user, agent ID for agent. Also decide: matching by FromID is authoritative since agent IDs are unique; FromName can be duplicated (agents can share names? Check CreateWithBackend whether name unique). Also Message.FromName could equal another agent's. Also `Route` / channel message from agent — check how agent sends messages to channel, what fromId/fromName are used.\n\nLet me check where SendToChannel is called for agent replies (channelID path in loop_backend: m.emit / something writes back to channel). Search for \"AddMessage\" / \"Broadcast\" callers and \"SendToChannel\" in frontend/main.go bindings.\n\nAlso important: Render(self) receiving fromName would be ambiguous — in SendToChannel, `fromName` is the sender of the current message, not the receiving agent! Big bug: Render(fromName) would mark the *sender's* messages as \"你自己\" for every recipient. That's a critical flaw in B. The right param is per-recipient agent identity, so it must move inside the loop over recipients (which is where prompt should be built per-recipient), or at minimum Render must be called per-agent with that agent's ID.\n\nThat's the killer finding: `prompt` is built once outside the loop (line 604) and shared by all recipients. So A+B as written labels the *speaker's* earlier turns as \"你自己\" for everyone — exactly inverted. Wait: in the self-repeat scenario, agent X repeats itself; the current message is from user or another agent. If the last message is from agent Y and recipients include Y and X, then prompt built with fromName=Y marks Y's messages as \"你自己\" for X too. Also when the current message is sent by the agent itself (does that happen? no, recipients exclude sender per Broadcast line 351). But for other recipients, sender's own speech is marked \"你自己\" — false. So需调整: move prompt construction into per-recipient loop with agent's ID.\n\n3. Which field: FromID. Evidence: Broadcast(channelId, fromId, fromName, text) — fromId used for exclusion (line 351 `memberId != fromId`), so fromId is the canonical identity; FromName is display only. Also ShouldReply compares mentions against agentName OR agentID (line 413) — showing that names are not unique/authoritative. Also role member: `channel.Member{ID: sn.ID, Name: sn.Name...}`. So Render should take selfID string and match m.FromID == selfID. But careful: user messages have FromID \"user\"; if selfID == \"user\" (when API is called for a user?) SendToChannel fromId is \"user\" for user. So need guard: never mark FromID==\"user\" as self even if... actually if selfID is user, that's not an agent case. Fine.\n\nLet me check whether agent names can duplicate and how agent posts to channel (to confirm FromID is agent ID).\n\n4. What about transcript truncation dropping head: BuildTranscript keeps last-N messages within 2000 chars; it can drop the earliest messages while keeping later ones — meaning the \"self repeats\" pattern may still occur. Also note BuildTranscript's budget accounting: size = len(FromName)+len(Text)+20, and it breaks when exceeded but len(out)\u003e0, so it always includes at least one message; single huge message can exceed budget. Also, adding \"## 现在需要你回应的消息\" fence — if transcript is empty (Render returns \"\"), and prompt is `fmt.Sprintf(\"%s\\n%s: %s\", ...)` — with the fence the empty case yields fence presumably. Also A's fence placement: the final message is outside Render (the current `text` is appended after Render), so \"末尾加围栏\" means fence at end of Render output, then the current message follows — so the fence header appears *after* the label but before the current message only if Render writes it last. Actually if Render writes \"## 现在需要你回应的消息\\n\" at the end, then prompt becomes \"...fence\\nfrom: text\" — that works. But note transcript already includes `## 群聊上下文` header. Fine.\n\nBut subtlety: since transcript excludes the current message (main.go:577-579), self's own past turns are the only source of self-repeat. Marking them 「你自己」 helps. However if the current message from agent Y contains a quote of X's earlier text... eh.\n\n5. Other unaddressed scenarios:\n- Same agent @ twice consecutively: irrelevant for prompt, but \"只回应最后一条\" conflicts with the fact that transcript already excludes the current message; the \"only reply to last message\" instruction is about the fence. If two different agents are @-ed in one message, each gets the same prompt; fine.\n- The real overlap: a.Messages personal history (loop_backend.go:42-45 `history := append(...a.Messages...)`) plus transcript both contain the agent's own prior replies → duplicate self content in one conversation. And after run, a.Messages = out.Messages, which now includes the whole rendered transcript as user content; next round history includes the older transcript; so transcript chunks accumulate in personal history — the \"## 群聊上下文\" block from previous turn stays in history. So over turns, the model sees repeated nested transcripts → self-repetition reinforcing. Neither A nor C removes that; C is just a prompt soft constraint. Actually this is the crux: the transcript splice into user message gets persisted into a.Messages via runner Sync callback (loop_backend.go:87-92) and `a.Messages = out.Messages` (line ~121). So each turn's transcript becomes permanent history, and each new turn's transcript re-includes what the agent already said in history. Duplication grows: turn n has n-1 embedded transcript blocks. That's a real finding — a stronger root cause than A addresses.\n\n- Budget: BuildTranscript budget 2000 measured by chars but the Message from loop_backend prefix.\n\n- Also: does channel message get written back to channel and then that message becomes part of next transcript? Yes.\n\n- Another scenario the fix can't solve: two agents with the same FromName — matching by name marks other agent's lines as yours. And if agent's name is a substring... no.\n\n- Truncation dropping head: if truncation cuts in middle, the fence says \"现在需要你回应的消息\" but the head-cut transcript may begin mid-thread; also BuildTranscript may drop the *current* context after? no, keeps tail.\n\n- Also `Render` marks self but the agent's own messages are also in a.Messages history as \"assistant\" role — so the prompt now says 「你自己」 in user text while history shows them as real assistant turns, giving the model a doubled, differently-labeled copy. Could encourage echoing.\n\n6. More fundamental fix: map transcript into real multi-role messages. Feasibility: PromptRequest has Text string only? Check agent.PromptRequest fields — Text, ChannelID, AgentID. Would need to add Messages []llm.Message or a Transcript field to PromptRequest, thread through m.Prompt → run functions. Let's examine manager.Prompt and PromptRequest to estimate change surface.\n\nLet me check main.go AgentView/PromptRequest struct definitions and manager.Prompt."
