        "timestamp": 1794915802,
        "reasoning": "Confirmed: manager.go:752 appends req.Text (containing the whole rendered transcript + current line) to a.Messages as a user message. And executeLoop copies a.Messages as starting history (loop_backend.go:42-45) and resets a.Messages to out.Messages (line ~120). So the rendered transcript is persisted as conversation history permanently. That is the actual root cause of growing duplication: each new turn embeds a new full transcript copy, and history already contains older copies — an O(n²) duplication.\n\nNote interesting: manager.go:752 appends the user message BEFORE executeLoop reads a.Messages — so history already includes the current prompt (with transcript). Then executeLoop takes `history := a.Messages` and builds loop.State{Messages: history}. Meanwhile manager.go:752 already added the current prompt. OK.\n\nAlso verify: in executeLoop, `st.Messages = history` — includes the transcript-containing user message. Then the runner appends assistant reply. Then a.Messages = out.Messages. So yes.\n\nNow, does the mock backend do the same? yes.\n\nNow let me quantify the duplication risk and also note that the current-utterance duplicate line 604: prompt = transcript + \"\\n\" + fromName+\": \"+text — but transcript already excludes the current message (main.go:575-577), so no duplication there. Good. However if transcript is empty, prompt starts with \"\\n你: hello\" (leading blank line) — minor.\n\nWait — a subtle bug in the exclusion: `prior := ch.Messages; if len(prior)\u003e0 \u0026\u0026 prior[len(prior)-1].ID == msg.ID { prior = prior[:len(prior)-1] }`. ch is a clone from Get, so slicing is safe.\n\nBut under concurrency: if another message lands between Broadcast and Get, the last message might not be msg → exclusion fails → the current text appears twice (once in transcript, once as the tail). That's a real race — screenshot: two agents replying concurrently... actually agent replies go via AddMessage from event handler goroutine, so yes concurrent appends are possible. So duplicate-current-message is possible. Render(self) wouldn't fix that.\n\nNow, let me check if there's any test asserting exact prompt or \"只回应最后一条\" etc. And check e2e specs mentioning repeat.\n\nAlso worth checking: does the frontend display agent self-repetition? Not needed.\n\nNow let's evaluate the \"more fundamental fix\": map transcript to real messages. Feasibility analysis:\n- PromptRequest (manager.go:529-539) is a JSON-bound struct (json tags), part of the public API boundary (main.go:47 type alias, mygo bindings?). Adding a field with `json:\"-\"` is safe: `History []llm.Message` or `Context []channel.Message`. But agent package importing channel package → import cycle? channel imports nothing from agent (channel.go imports encoding/json, fmt, os, strings, sync, time) — safe direction: agent → channel. But cleaner: define a neutral type in agent package, e.g. `type PeerMessage struct{ FromID, FromName, Text string }`, and have main.go convert channel.Message → agent.PeerMessage. That keeps agent package free of channel dependency.\n- Then in execute (manager.go:746-752): instead of a single user message, append multiple messages: map each transcript message to {Role: \"user\" if FromID != self else \"assistant\", Content: name + \": \" + text}. Mind: llm.Message likely has Role/Content/ToolID/Timestamp/Reasoning. Let me confirm llm.Message fields.\n- Danger: messagesExtend (loop_backend.go:213-225) prefix check compares Role/Content/ToolID — inserting history changes checkpoint resume semantics; resumable/checkpoint state stored per agent ID (runID = a.ID) so old checkpoints would be considered non-extending → new run. That's fine (falls back to fresh).\n- Danger: `a.Messages` also feeds the UI (manager.go:174, 1164, 1192 \"messages\" snapshot) — if we insert transcript as assistant/user messages, the desktop chat view would display the group transcript inside the agent's single-chat history. That's a UI regression. Need to keep UI-visible history separate from model-facing context. That's the main cost: two histories (display vs model). Currently they're the same field.\n- Danger: ToolID/tool_call pairing — inserting fake assistant messages without tool calls between tool results could break providers that require strict alternation. Mitigate: insert only plain text messages, and place them at the point of the current user turn, i.e., before appending the current user message. Also the assistant self-messages must not carry tool calls.\n- Cost estimate: PromptRequest field + execute() mapping (~30 lines) + loop_backend/mock/CLI all use a.Messages, so it propagates nearly for free (since execute appends into a.Messages before backend runs) — actually inserting into a.Messages affects UI. Alternative minimal: append the context messages only inside the backend's local `history` slice, not into a.Messages. Then a.Messages retains the single user text for UI... but then the model history and stored history diverge, and a.Messages = out.Messages at loop_backend.go:120 would blow away... out.Messages includes the injected context → it would end up persisted anyway. So we'd need to strip.\n\nSo the \"fundamental fix\" cost: medium-large, touching agent/manager.go execute, loop_backend, mock/CLI paths, plus separating UI-visible messages. Realistic recommendation: phase it — Phase 1: per-recipient Render(selfID) + dedupe; Phase 2: structured messages with history-replacement instead of splice-accumulate (i.e., don't persist the rendered transcript into a.Messages at all: for channel turns, build the context fresh from the channel each turn and store only the agent's own reply in a.Messages). That last one is the key insight and a smaller change: in execute(), if req.ChannelID != \"\" (and/or a flag like req.Ephemeral/ContextOnly), don't append req.Text to a.Messages permanently — instead append to a transient history used for this run only. That fixes the O(n²) growth and stops transcript blocks accumulating.\n\nLet me verify llm.Message fields to make the recommendation accurate."
